1
00:00:00,700 --> 00:00:03,800
This is a close look at
one part of SEAVISTA,

2
00:00:03,800 --> 00:00:07,533
a research project built
at FORTH-ICS and co-funded

3
00:00:07,533 --> 00:00:12,301
by Frontex under the Frontex
Research Grants Programme.

4
00:00:12,301 --> 00:00:17,240
Picture a busy sea on an ordinary day.
Thousands of ships, all on the move.

5
00:00:17,240 --> 00:00:20,133
Our example is the waters around Denmark.

6
00:00:20,133 --> 00:00:22,995
Any other sea area can
be added the same way.

7
00:00:22,995 --> 00:00:26,167
We want a system that keeps
up with all of that traffic,

8
00:00:26,167 --> 00:00:29,679
and spots the one vessel
that behaves unlike the rest.

9
00:00:29,679 --> 00:00:31,100
You could write rules.

10
00:00:31,100 --> 00:00:34,536
But a rule only catches what
somebody thought of in advance.

11
00:00:34,536 --> 00:00:37,367
You could learn from
examples of bad behaviour.

12
00:00:37,367 --> 00:00:40,263
But there are very few,
and they keep changing.

13
00:00:40,263 --> 00:00:41,833
So we turn it around.

14
00:00:41,833 --> 00:00:44,233
We teach the system
what normal looks like,

15
00:00:44,233 --> 00:00:46,943
and anything far from normal stands out.

16
00:00:46,943 --> 00:00:51,015
And when something stands
out, a person takes a look.

17
00:00:51,015 --> 00:00:55,950
Learning normal sounds simple. Show the
computer lots of ships, and let it learn.

18
00:00:55,950 --> 00:00:57,733
But look at these two ships.

19
00:00:57,733 --> 00:01:02,291
They make the same turn, one here,
and one far away, heading another way.

20
00:01:02,291 --> 00:01:07,267
To a computer reading positions, these
look like two completely different things.

21
00:01:07,267 --> 00:01:09,967
It would have to learn the
same turn over and over,

22
00:01:09,967 --> 00:01:12,292
once for every place and direction.

23
00:01:12,292 --> 00:01:17,233
So we slide both to the same starting
point, and turn them to face the same way.

24
00:01:17,233 --> 00:01:19,814
Now they are plainly the same turn.

25
00:01:19,814 --> 00:01:21,167
Done for every hour,

26
00:01:21,167 --> 00:01:23,533
this means each example of a manoeuvre,

27
00:01:23,533 --> 00:01:24,767
wherever it happened,

28
00:01:24,767 --> 00:01:27,076
teaches the model the same lesson.

29
00:01:27,076 --> 00:01:29,800
And we split normal
into separate questions.

30
00:01:29,800 --> 00:01:31,267
Is the movement normal?

31
00:01:31,267 --> 00:01:33,767
Does it follow from what
the ship was just doing?

32
00:01:33,767 --> 00:01:36,073
Is it somewhere ships actually go?

33
00:01:36,073 --> 00:01:41,700
Each question gets its own score.
Adding another question later is easy.

34
00:01:41,700 --> 00:01:44,457
Start with the first
question, the movement.

35
00:01:44,457 --> 00:01:47,500
A ship's track is a
long list of positions.

36
00:01:47,500 --> 00:01:49,200
Ships report at different rates,

37
00:01:49,200 --> 00:01:51,300
so no two lists are the same length,

38
00:01:51,300 --> 00:01:53,431
and that makes them hard to compare.

39
00:01:53,431 --> 00:01:56,611
So we cut every voyage
into one hour pieces.

40
00:01:56,611 --> 00:02:01,253
Then we squeeze each piece down to eight
numbers, a fingerprint of the manoeuvre.

41
00:02:01,253 --> 00:02:03,000
Why can eight numbers be enough?

42
00:02:03,000 --> 00:02:05,433
Because a real track is a simple shape.

43
00:02:05,433 --> 00:02:09,009
A ship turns gently, and
its speed changes slowly.

44
00:02:09,009 --> 00:02:11,967
So real hours take only
a few kinds of shape,

45
00:02:11,967 --> 00:02:15,454
and eight numbers are
enough to tell them apart.

46
00:02:15,454 --> 00:02:18,565
Here is how one hour
becomes those eight numbers.

47
00:02:18,565 --> 00:02:21,200
First, we move it so it starts at zero,

48
00:02:21,200 --> 00:02:24,685
and turn it so the ship always
starts out heading the same way.

49
00:02:24,685 --> 00:02:28,610
Every hour now starts in the same
place, pointing the same way.

50
00:02:28,610 --> 00:02:33,069
Next, we swap the jumpy list of
positions for one smooth curve.

51
00:02:33,069 --> 00:02:36,367
The curve is built by adding
up a few standard wave shapes,

52
00:02:36,367 --> 00:02:38,578
called Chebyshev polynomials.

53
00:02:38,578 --> 00:02:41,800
Sixteen of them for
east, sixteen for north.

54
00:02:41,800 --> 00:02:45,427
Thirty two numbers in all,
however many reports the hour had.

55
00:02:45,427 --> 00:02:47,267
Then a small neural network,

56
00:02:47,267 --> 00:02:48,633
an autoencoder,

57
00:02:48,633 --> 00:02:51,600
learns to squeeze those
thirty two numbers into eight,

58
00:02:51,600 --> 00:02:54,325
and to rebuild the curve from them.

59
00:02:54,325 --> 00:02:57,367
Now we need to say how normal
those eight numbers are.

60
00:02:57,367 --> 00:02:59,743
For that, we need a probability.

61
00:02:59,743 --> 00:03:02,909
We use a technique called
a normalizing flow.

62
00:03:02,909 --> 00:03:05,267
It starts with the
simplest shape there is,

63
00:03:05,267 --> 00:03:08,167
a bell curve, and learns
to stretch and bend it,

64
00:03:08,167 --> 00:03:11,046
step by step, until it matches the data.

65
00:03:11,046 --> 00:03:14,893
Here it is in one dimension.
One bell becomes two bumps.

66
00:03:14,893 --> 00:03:19,373
In two dimensions, a round blob
learns to become two crescent moons.

67
00:03:19,373 --> 00:03:22,367
And on real ship positions
in the Danish straits,

68
00:03:22,367 --> 00:03:24,690
it learns the shipping lanes on its own.

69
00:03:24,690 --> 00:03:28,133
Because every bend can be
undone, it works both ways.

70
00:03:28,133 --> 00:03:31,100
Give it a ship, and it
says how likely that is.

71
00:03:31,100 --> 00:03:34,636
Run it backwards, and it
produces new, plausible ones.

72
00:03:34,636 --> 00:03:36,833
And because it is a true probability,

73
00:03:36,833 --> 00:03:39,367
a score means the same
thing for every ship,

74
00:03:39,367 --> 00:03:41,106
and scores can be added.

75
00:03:41,106 --> 00:03:44,500
Give it an ordinary hour,
and it says very likely.

76
00:03:44,500 --> 00:03:48,387
Give it a strange one,
and the probability drops.

77
00:03:48,387 --> 00:03:51,022
The second question is about sequence.

78
00:03:51,022 --> 00:03:55,560
Each hour can look normal on its own,
while the order they come in does not.

79
00:03:55,560 --> 00:04:00,208
So a second flow learns what usually
comes next, given the hour before.

80
00:04:00,208 --> 00:04:02,467
This is a conditional probability.

81
00:04:02,467 --> 00:04:06,000
How likely the next hour is,
given the one we just saw.

82
00:04:06,000 --> 00:04:09,300
Run it forwards, and it
imagines the next hour.

83
00:04:09,300 --> 00:04:13,285
Ask it many times, and you get
a whole fan of possible futures.

84
00:04:13,285 --> 00:04:17,674
Here is what the ship actually
did. It falls right inside the fan.

85
00:04:17,674 --> 00:04:20,600
And the condition can hold
more than the last hour.

86
00:04:20,600 --> 00:04:25,438
The traffic around it, the water, the
ports, the destination it broadcasts.

87
00:04:25,438 --> 00:04:28,167
Each one changes what counts as normal.

88
00:04:28,167 --> 00:04:33,550
The same turn can be routine near
a port, and unusual in open water.

89
00:04:33,550 --> 00:04:35,622
The third question is where.

90
00:04:35,622 --> 00:04:38,567
To learn movement, we threw location away.

91
00:04:38,567 --> 00:04:41,554
So now we bring it back,
as a score of its own.

92
00:04:41,554 --> 00:04:45,864
A third flow learns where ships are
found, and that they are never on land.

93
00:04:45,864 --> 00:04:49,961
Take the same hour and move it
thirty kilometres into empty water.

94
00:04:49,961 --> 00:04:55,254
The movement score stays exactly
where it was. The place score jumps.

95
00:04:55,254 --> 00:04:58,800
Each question is a separate
module, trained on its own.

96
00:04:58,800 --> 00:05:00,921
New checks can be plugged in later.

97
00:05:00,921 --> 00:05:05,448
They can also take extra information,
like whether the ship is near a port.

98
00:05:05,448 --> 00:05:07,967
A raw probability is hard to read.

99
00:05:07,967 --> 00:05:10,400
So each score becomes one plain number.

100
00:05:10,400 --> 00:05:14,600
Out of a hundred ordinary hours, how
many look stranger than this one?

101
00:05:14,600 --> 00:05:19,328
This stage is meant to be cautious,
so that nothing unusual slips past.

102
00:05:19,328 --> 00:05:25,312
An AI agent then investigates each
one, and a person makes the final call.

103
00:05:25,312 --> 00:05:28,194
All of this runs on a single workstation.

104
00:05:28,194 --> 00:05:33,133
One request at a time, it handles about
six thousand vessel updates a second.

105
00:05:33,133 --> 00:05:36,015
Grouped into batches,
forty eight times that.

106
00:05:36,015 --> 00:05:39,732
And there is a lot of room
left to make it faster.

107
00:05:39,732 --> 00:05:45,867
SEAVISTA was built at FORTH-ICS and CVRL.
